# REVISION-FRONT-OFFICE-BRANCH-CONTEXT-LOCK-1

## The two named invariants

    BRANCH CONTEXT LOCK != DEVICE PROOF REQUIREMENT
    DEVICE LOCK IMPLIES BRANCH PIN

They are separate security layers with different blast radii and MUST be armable
independently. Conflating them is the exact defect this revision removed.

    branchPinApplies(user)    = in cohort AND (context_lock ON OR device_lock ON)
    deviceProofRequired(user) = in cohort AND device_lock ON

## Predicate ownership — do not cross the streams

| Question | Ask ONLY | Flag |
|---|---|---|
| Is this account's branch pinned? | `FrontOfficeBranchPinResolver` | either |
| Must this account prove a device? | `FrontOfficeBranchDeviceLockService` | device only |

`BranchContext::forUser()` and `UserOnlineContextService::assertFrontOfficeBranchLock()`
ask the PIN resolver. The login controller, `EnsureFrontOfficeDeviceSession` and the
WebAuthn controller ask the DEVICE service. Those two sets must never swap: the
separation is structural, which is why the branch-context path references no
device symbol at all.

Turning on `front_office.branch_context_lock` alone MUST NOT cause a credential
lookup, an assertion ceremony, a device-cookie requirement, or a login denial for
a missing credential. There is a test that proves the branch-context path issues
no query against `mst_doctor_devices`, `trx_doctor_device_webauthn_credentials`
or `mst_doctor_device_authorizations`.

Arming the device flag still pins the branch. Do not "simplify" the `OR` in
`pinningEnabled()` to read only the context flag — that silently retracts a
promise the already-GO device capability made.

## The pin must reach EVERY branch authority

`BranchContext::forUser()` is NOT the only authority on a working branch.
`resolveActiveBranchForAdmin()` (the branch a NEW CLINIC VISIT is registered at)
and `RmeWorkingBranchScope` read `activeContextBranchId()` directly. Pinning only
`BranchContext` produced a split brain that shipped undetected in the device-lock
sprint: the pin read as narrowing while visits were still created on the wider
branch.

The pin therefore lives in `activeContextBranchId()` — the one chokepoint they
share. Do not add a new branch reader that bypasses it, and do not "simplify" the
narrowing out of it.

**A conflict resolves to NULL, never to the pin.** Returning the pin would
resurrect a context the daily branch lock already decided and grant a same-day
branch move. Null is safe for registration: `StoreClinicVisitRequest::authorize()`
refuses when it is null rather than trusting a form `branch_id`.

`BranchChangeApprovalService` is the only other writer of a context `branch_id`;
it refuses to move a pinned account, at request and at approval.

## Scope is the cohort, never the role

`FrontOfficeBranchDeviceCohort` is the ONLY user-to-branch source of truth: one
env key, ceiling of four, allowlist `TLK1 LDK2 ATG3 SPN4`. Do not add a second
cohort, a second config, a `users.branch_id` backfill, or a
`hasRole('Front Office')` shortcut — production carries EIGHT Front Office
accounts and only FOUR are approved.

The four out-of-scope accounts are unchanged because they are ABSENT from the
cohort. Never add an allowlist exception naming them; that inverts the rule.

`users.branch_id` is NOT an enforcement boundary — it sits BELOW the online
context in `BranchContext`, so setting it cannot stop a branch switch.

## Fail closed means "no branch", not "no pin"

An account that is ARMED but UNDECIDABLE (duplicate entry, off-policy branch
code, oversized cohort, or a pinned branch that lost `is_active` /
`is_rme_enabled`) resolves to **NULL**, not to the ordinary fallback chain.

The device layer could treat this as "do not pin" because such an account had
already been denied login. The branch-context layer never denies a login, so
falling through would hand the account its online context, its `users.branch_id`
or MAIN — every one WIDER than intended. That is fail OPEN and is forbidden.

A token naming no user id at all (`abc:SPN4`) is a config error and puts NOBODY
in scope — it must not fail anybody closed.

## The refusal lives where the WRITE happens, not only on the route

`StoreClinicVisitRequest::authorize()` returns `true` for everyone EXCEPT a pinned
account whose working branch is null. Do not "simplify" that back to `return true`.

Why it is needed: `applyAdminClinicBranchContext()` early-returns when the working
branch is null, leaving the form's own `branch_id` to validate against ANY
RME-enabled branch. `EnsureRmeOnlineContext` does intercept first — but a route
guard must not be the only thing between a crafted POST and the write.

**Do not read `isAdminClinicRegistration()` as an authorization gate.** It is a
private helper deciding whether to APPLY the context branch. It contains a
`resolveActiveBranchForAdmin(...) !== null` that looks exactly like an authorize
check and is not one. Misreading it produced a false safety claim in a shipped
docblock during this sprint.

## resolveActiveBranchForAdmin() is NOT the working branch

It answers for the admin_clinic and perawat contexts only, and returns null for a
doctor or kasir context even when the working branch resolves fine. Never use it to
decide whether an operator HAS a working branch — that is `activeContextBranchId()`,
which is also the pin-narrowed, daily-lock-aware one. Using the former in
`StoreClinicVisitRequest::authorize()` produced a false 403 for a dual-role cohort
account online at its own pinned branch.

Swapping it for `activeContextBranchId()` in `authorize()` ALONE is also wrong:
`applyAdminClinicBranchContext()` still early-returns for such a context, so the
form's `branch_id` survives. Both the authorize check and
`applyFrontOfficePinnedBranchContext()` are required — and the forced value must be
`activeContextBranchId()`, never `requiredBranchIdFor()`, which would bypass the
daily lock.

## Both halves of a narrowing must be applied

A controller that computes a restriction and a blade that recomputes its own list
is one fix, half-applied. The selector's misconfigured case must outrank
`$dailyLocked`, and an empty branch list needs a visible refusal — not an empty
`<select>`.

## UI is not the boundary

A one-option selector refuses nothing. The server-side assert in
`UserOnlineContextService` and the pin in `BranchContext::forUser()` are the
boundary; the selector narrowing exists only so an operator is not offered a
choice that would fail on submit.

## The pin does not outrank the daily branch lock

`FEATURE-DAILY-BRANCH-CONTEXT-LOCK-1` and the pin are two INDEPENDENT narrowing
rules. The pin must never become a way around the daily lock — that would hand an
armed account a same-day branch move the daily lock requires approval for.

Consequence: arming an account mid-day, when it has ALREADY committed today to a
different branch, leaves it unable to select anything until the clinical day
rolls over. That is a runbook pre-flight check, not something to "fix" by letting
the pin win. There is a test pinning this.

## Activation

Both flags ship OFF with an EMPTY committed cohort. Arming is a supervised
per-account ceremony, never a deploy. Because the two layers SHARE the cohort,
arming an account for branch context and later arming the device flag will
additionally demand WebAuthn from that same account — plan that deliberately.
